<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-circle-trigonometry">
  <title>Circle Trigonometry</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-circle-trigonometry">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain why the opposite vertex of a unit right triangle with a vertex at the origin has coordinates <m>(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta))</m>, and connect the Pythagorean theorem to the unit circle equation <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Locate the terminal ray of any angle by rotating from <m>(1,0)</m>, counterclockwise for positive angles and clockwise for negative angles, and use its point on the unit circle to define <m>\sin(\theta)</m> and <m>\cos(\theta)</m> for every angle.</p></li>
      <li><p>Define <m>\tan(\theta)</m>, <m>\sec(\theta)</m>, <m>\cot(\theta)</m>, and <m>\csc(\theta)</m> as ratios of the coordinates, and decide where each is undefined.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find all six trigonometric values of an angle by combining a reference triangle with the signs of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in the quadrant of the terminal ray.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> and the quadrant to find all six trigonometric values when only one coordinate is known.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain why sine is an odd function and cosine is an even function, using the symmetry of the unit circle.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time we rescaled right triangles so that the hypotenuse had length <m>1</m>, and found
      that the other two sides are <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)</m>. That works only for
      acute angles, because a right triangle cannot have an angle of <m>150^\circ</m> or
      <m>-40^\circ</m>. Today we place the unit triangle inside a circle. The circle will let us
      turn what we observed for angles between <m>0^\circ</m> and <m>90^\circ</m> into a
      <em>definition</em> that works for every angle.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-unit-triangles-in-circle" workspace="0in">
    <title>Unit Triangles Around the Origin</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The figure shows several right triangles whose hypotenuse has length <m>1</m>. Each has
        one vertex at the origin <m>(0,0)</m> and one leg along the <m>x</m>-axis. The vertex
        opposite the origin is a point <m>P=(x,y)</m> on the circle of radius <m>r=1</m> centered at
        the origin, called the <term>unit circle</term>. The highlighted triangle has the acute
        angle <m>\theta</m> at the origin.
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
          The first quadrant of the unit circle with x and y axes. Three right triangles have a
          vertex at the origin, a hypotenuse of length 1 along a radius to the circle, and a
          vertical leg dropping to the x-axis. The middle triangle is highlighted. Its angle at the
          origin is theta, its horizontal leg is labeled cosine of theta, its vertical leg is
          labeled sine of theta, and its vertex on the circle is labeled with the coordinates
          cosine of theta, sine of theta. The point 1, 0 is marked on the x-axis.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=5]
            \draw[->] (-0.05,0) -- (1.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->] (0,-0.05) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {$y$};
            \draw[thick] (1,0) arc (0:90:1);
            \foreach \a in {25,75} {
              \draw[gray] (0,0) -- ({cos(\a)},{sin(\a)}) -- ({cos(\a)},0);
              \fill[gray] ({cos(\a)},{sin(\a)}) circle (0.012);
              \draw[gray] ({cos(\a)-0.04},0) -- ({cos(\a)-0.04},0.04) -- ({cos(\a)},0.04);
            }
            \draw[thick, blue] (0,0) -- ({cos(50)},{sin(50)}) -- ({cos(50)},0) -- cycle;
            \draw[blue] ({cos(50)-0.04},0) -- ({cos(50)-0.04},0.04) -- ({cos(50)},0.04);
            \fill[blue] ({cos(50)},{sin(50)}) circle (0.015);
            \fill (1,0) circle (0.015);
            \node[below right] at (1,0) {$(1,0)$};
            \draw[blue] (0.2,0) arc (0:50:0.2);
            \node[blue] at (0.27,0.1) {$\theta$};
            \node[blue, above left] at ({0.5*cos(50)},{0.5*sin(50)}) {$1$};
            \node[blue, below] at ({cos(50)},-0.01) {$\cos(\theta)$};
            \node[blue, right] at ({cos(50)},{0.5*sin(50)}) {$\sin(\theta)$};
            \node[blue, above right] at ({cos(50)},{sin(50)}) {$P=(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta))$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-vertex-coordinates" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the unit triangle with hypotenuse <m>1</m> from the previous activity, what are the
          lengths of the horizontal and vertical legs of the highlighted triangle? Explain why the
          point <m>P</m> has coordinates <m>(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta))</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-circle-equation" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply the Pythagorean theorem to the highlighted triangle to get an equation relating
          the coordinates <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> of <m>P</m>. What familiar equation is this? Which
          identity from the previous activity does it give when written using
          <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-angle-zero" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to the triangle as <m>\theta</m> gets smaller and smaller? Which point on the
          circle should we assign to the angle <m>\theta=0^\circ</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-arc-length" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Moving along the circle from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>P</m> covers a distance called the
          <term>arc length</term>. This distance is a fraction of the full circumference
          <m>2\pi r=2\pi</m>, and the fraction is the same as the fraction of a full revolution
          (<m>360^\circ</m>) that the angle covers. Find the arc length for
          <m>\theta=90^\circ</m> and for <m>\theta=45^\circ</m>. Then write a formula for the arc
          length when the angle is <m>\theta</m> degrees.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <definition xml:id="def-uc-trig-functions">
    <title>Trigonometric Functions of Any Angle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        To find the point for an angle <m>\theta</m>, start at <m>(1,0)</m> on the unit circle and
        move along the circle to cover the angle. If <m>\theta\gt 0</m>, rotate in the
        counterclockwise direction. If <m>\theta\lt 0</m>, rotate in the clockwise direction through
        <m>|\theta|</m>. The ray from <m>(0,0)</m> through the point reached is the
        <term>terminal ray</term> of the angle. If the point is <m>(x,y)</m>, then we
        <em>define</em>
        <md>
          <mrow> \cos(\theta) \amp = x \amp \sin(\theta) \amp = y </mrow>
        </md>
        and define the other four trigonometric functions as ratios of these coordinates:
        <md>
          <mrow> \tan(\theta) \amp = \frac{y}{x} = \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)} \amp \sec(\theta) \amp = \frac{1}{x} = \frac{1}{\cos(\theta)} </mrow>
          <mrow> \cot(\theta) \amp = \frac{x}{y} = \frac{\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)} \amp \csc(\theta) \amp = \frac{1}{y} = \frac{1}{\sin(\theta)} </mrow>
        </md>
        whenever the denominator is not zero.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-uc-agrees-with-triangles">
    <title>Reversing Our Observation</title>
    <p>
      For an acute angle, this definition agrees with the right triangle ratios, because we just
      saw that the point is <m>(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta))</m> when <m>\theta</m> is acute. The
      difference is the direction of the logic. Before, the triangle defined the coordinates. Now the
      coordinates define sine and cosine, and they do so for angles of any size or sign. Every point
      on the unit circle satisfies <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, so
      <md>\cos^2(\theta)+\sin^2(\theta)=1</md>
      holds for <em>every</em> angle <m>\theta</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-quadrant-signs" workspace="0in">
    <title>Signs in the Four Quadrants</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The coordinate axes divide the plane into four <term>quadrants</term>, numbered I through IV
        counterclockwise, with Quadrant I in the upper right.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-sign-chart" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each quadrant, decide whether <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are positive or negative for a
          point in that quadrant. Use this to decide the sign of <m>\cos(\theta)</m>,
          <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, and <m>\tan(\theta)</m> when the terminal ray of <m>\theta</m> is in
          that quadrant.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-which-quadrant" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In which quadrant is the terminal ray for each of the angles <m>150^\circ</m>,
          <m>200^\circ</m>, <m>-40^\circ</m>, and <m>400^\circ</m>? Describe how you decided for
          <m>400^\circ</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-uc-special-triangles">
    <title>Two Special Triangles</title>
    <p>
      Two right triangles with hypotenuse <m>1</m> have side lengths that we can find exactly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>The <m>45^\circ</m>-<m>45^\circ</m>-<m>90^\circ</m> triangle.</em> The two acute angles
      are equal, so the triangle is isosceles and both legs have the same length <m>x</m>. The
      Pythagorean theorem gives <m>x^2+x^2=1</m>, so <m>x^2=\dfrac{1}{2}</m> and
      <m>x=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>The <m>30^\circ</m>-<m>60^\circ</m>-<m>90^\circ</m> triangle.</em> An equilateral
      triangle with side length <m>1</m> can have one angle bisected to create an angle of <m>30^\circ</m>.
      The bisector cuts the opposite side in half, so the short leg of each right triangle is
      <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m>. The Pythagorean theorem then gives the long leg
      <m>\sqrt{1-\dfrac{1}{4}}=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="35% 35%" valign="middle">
      <image>
        <shortdescription>
          A right triangle with the right angle at the lower right. Both legs are labeled
          square root of 2 over 2, the hypotenuse is labeled 1, and both acute angles are labeled
          45 degrees.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3,0) -- (3,3) -- cycle;
            \draw (2.7,0) -- (2.7,0.3) -- (3,0.3);
            \draw (0.8,0) arc (0:45:0.8);
            \node at (1.2,0.22) {$45^\circ$};
            \draw (3,2.3) arc (270:225:0.7);
            \node at (2.45,2.0) {$45^\circ$};
            \node[below] at (1.5,0) {$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$};
            \node[right] at (3,1.5) {$\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$};
            \node[above left] at (1.5,1.5) {$1$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
      <image>
        <shortdescription>
          An equilateral triangle with side length 1 and one vertex at the origin on the left. A
          horizontal dashed line from the origin bisects the angle there, with the upper half
          labeled 30 degrees and the lower half labeled negative 30 degrees. The two slanted sides
          are labeled 1. The vertical side on the right is cut by the bisector into two pieces,
          each labeled one half, and the bisector is labeled square root of 3 over 2. A right
          angle is marked where the bisector meets the vertical side.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=4]
            \pgfmathsetmacro{\c}{sqrt(3)/2}
            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (\c,0.5) -- (\c,-0.5) -- cycle;
            \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (\c,0);
            \draw (\c-0.07,0) -- (\c-0.07,0.07) -- (\c,0.07);
            \draw (0.3,0) arc (0:30:0.3);
            \draw (0.3,0) arc (0:-30:0.3);
            \node at (0.2,0.05) {$30^\circ$};
            \node at (0.77,0.35) {$60^\circ$};
            \node[above left] at (\c/2,0.25) {$1$};
            \node[below left] at (\c/2,-0.25) {$1$};
            \node[right] at (\c,0.25) {$\frac{1}{2}$};
            \node[right] at (\c,-0.25) {$\frac{1}{2}$};
            \node[above] at (0.55,0.0) {};
            \node[below] at (\c/2+0.05,0) {$\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-uc-example-150">
    <title>Example: The Angle 150 Degrees</title>
    <p>
      Find <m>\sin(150^\circ)</m> and <m>\cos(150^\circ)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The angle <m>150^\circ</m> is not acute, so it is not an angle of a right triangle. Instead,
      we rotate <m>150^\circ</m> counterclockwise from <m>(1,0)</m> and mark the point
      <m>(x,y)</m> on the unit circle. This point is in Quadrant II. If we draw a vertical line from
      the point to the <m>x</m>-axis, we get a <term>reference triangle</term>. Its angle at the
      origin is <m>180^\circ-150^\circ=30^\circ</m>, so its horizontal leg has length
      <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m> and its vertical leg has length <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <image width="50%">
      <shortdescription>
        The unit circle with a terminal ray in the second quadrant at 150 degrees from the positive
        x-axis. A vertical segment drops from the point x, y on the circle to the negative x-axis,
        forming a reference triangle whose angle at the origin is 30 degrees. An arc marks the
        150 degree rotation from the positive x-axis.
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.4]
          \draw[->] (-1.3,0) -- (1.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
          \draw[->] (0,-1.3) -- (0,1.35) node[above] {$y$};
          \draw (0,0) circle (1);
          \draw[thick, blue] (0,0) -- ({cos(150)},{sin(150)}) -- ({cos(150)},0) -- cycle;
          \draw[blue] ({cos(150)+0.04},0) -- ({cos(150)+0.04},0.04) -- ({cos(150)},0.04);
          \fill[blue] ({cos(150)},{sin(150)}) circle (0.025);
          \node[blue, above left] at ({cos(150)},{sin(150)}) {$(x,y)$};
          \draw[->, red] (0.25,0) arc (0:150:0.25);
          \node[red] at (0.12,0.33) {$150^\circ$};
          \draw[blue] ({0.35*cos(150)},{0.35*sin(150)}) arc (150:180:0.35);
          \node[blue] at (-0.5,0.07) {$30^\circ$};
          \node[below right] at (1,0) {$(1,0)$};
          \node[blue, left] at ({0.5*cos(150)+0.05},{0.5*sin(150)+0.12}) {$1$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
    <p>
      In Quadrant II we have <m>x\lt 0</m> and <m>y\gt 0</m>, so
      <m>(x,y)=\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\dfrac{1}{2}\right)</m>. Therefore
      <m>\cos(150^\circ)=-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m> and <m>\sin(150^\circ)=\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-reference-triangles" workspace="0in">
    <title>Reference Triangles at Work</title>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-150-other-four" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <xref ref="def-uc-trig-functions"/> and the coordinates found in
          <xref ref="assemblage-uc-example-150" text="title"/> to find the other four trigonometric function
          values at <m>150^\circ</m>: <m>\tan(150^\circ)</m>, <m>\sec(150^\circ)</m>,
          <m>\cot(150^\circ)</m>, and <m>\csc(150^\circ)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-225-reference" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the unit circle, the terminal ray for <m>225^\circ</m>, and a reference triangle.
          What is the angle of the reference triangle at the origin? Find the coordinates of the
          point, and then <m>\cos(225^\circ)</m> and <m>\sin(225^\circ)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-one-coordinate-known" workspace="0in">
    <title>When Only One Coordinate Is Known</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        An angle <m>\theta</m> has its terminal ray in Quadrant IV, and <m>\cos(\theta)=0.6</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-find-y" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the unit circle equation and the quadrant to find <m>y=\sin(\theta)</m>. Be careful
          about the sign, and sketch the point on the unit circle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-all-six" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all six trigonometric function values of <m>\theta</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-not-special" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does this angle come from a <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>45^\circ</m>, or <m>60^\circ</m>
          reference triangle? Did that prevent you from finding its trigonometric values?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-uc-not-a-list">
    <title>What <q>Using the Unit Circle</q> Means</title>
    <p>
      When we say we are using the unit circle, we are <em>not</em> consulting a memorized list of
      points built from <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>45^\circ</m>, and <m>60^\circ</m> reference triangles.
      We are using three ideas together: the equation <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> for points on the circle, the
      definitions <m>\cos(\theta)=x</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)=y</m>, and the signs of
      <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> determined by the quadrant of the terminal ray. The special triangles
      are the cases where the side lengths happen to be especially simple.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-multiples-of-90" workspace="0in">
    <title>Multiples of 90 Degrees</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When the terminal ray lies along an axis, there is no reference triangle, but the point on
        the unit circle can be read directly from the picture.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-axis-table" workspace="2.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Make a table with columns <m>\theta</m>, <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, and
          <m>\tan(\theta)</m>. For each of the angles
          <m>\theta=0^\circ, 90^\circ, 180^\circ, 270^\circ, -90^\circ, -180^\circ, 450^\circ</m>,
          sketch the rotation, identify the point <m>(x,y)</m>, and fill in the row. Write
          <q>undefined</q> where a value does not exist.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-axis-undefined" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="def-uc-trig-functions"/>, explain why <m>\tan(90^\circ)</m> is undefined.
          Which of the six trigonometric functions are undefined at <m>0^\circ</m>? At
          <m>90^\circ</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-opposite-angles" workspace="0in">
    <title>Opposite Angles</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The points <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> on the unit circle below correspond to the angles
        <m>\theta</m> and <m>-\theta</m>. (The picture shows <m>\theta=40^\circ</m>, but the idea
        works for any angle.)
      </p>
      <image width="50%">
        <shortdescription>
          The unit circle with two radii. One goes up and to the right at an angle theta above the
          positive x-axis and ends at the labeled point P on the circle. The other goes down and to
          the right at an angle theta below the positive x-axis, an angle of negative theta, and
          ends at the labeled point Q on the circle.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.4]
            \draw[->] (-1.3,0) -- (1.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->] (0,-1.3) -- (0,1.35) node[above] {$y$};
            \draw (0,0) circle (1);
            \draw[thick, blue] (0,0) -- ({cos(40)},{sin(40)});
            \draw[thick, red] (0,0) -- ({cos(-40)},{sin(-40)});
            \fill[blue] ({cos(40)},{sin(40)}) circle (0.025);
            \fill[red] ({cos(-40)},{sin(-40)}) circle (0.025);
            \node[blue, above right] at ({cos(40)},{sin(40)}) {$P$};
            \node[red, below right] at ({cos(-40)},{sin(-40)}) {$Q$};
            \draw[->, blue] (0.3,0) arc (0:40:0.3);
            \node[blue] at (0.45,0.12) {$\theta$};
            \draw[->, red] (0.5,0) arc (0:-40:0.5);
            \node[red] at (0.68,-0.12) {$-\theta$};
            \node[below right] at (1,0) {$(1,0)$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-compare-p-q" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the coordinates of <m>P</m> as <m>(x,y)</m>. How are the coordinates of <m>Q</m>
          related to those of <m>P</m>? Explain using the symmetry of the circle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-uc-odd-even">
    <title>Sine Is Odd and Cosine Is Even</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every angle <m>\theta</m>,
        <md>
          <mrow> \sin(-\theta) \amp = -\sin(\theta) </mrow>
          <mrow> \cos(-\theta) \amp = \cos(\theta)\text{.} </mrow>
        </md>
        That is, <m>\sin</m> is an odd function and <m>\cos</m> is an even function.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-uc-using-symmetry" workspace="0in">
    <title>Using Odd and Even</title>
    <task xml:id="task-uc-negative-150" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <xref ref="thm-uc-odd-even"/> and the values at <m>150^\circ</m> to find
          <m>\sin(-150^\circ)</m> and <m>\cos(-150^\circ)</m>. In which quadrant is the terminal ray
          for <m>-150^\circ</m>, and do the signs agree with your answer?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
